      "article_id": "65c849136af50052588f9015771eec44",
      "link": "https://www.manilatimes.net/2026/10/28/world/americas-emea/terrorist-groups-in-syria-hit-after-jordan-church-attack/2455040",
      "title": "\u2018Terrorist\u2019 groups in Syria hit after Jordan church attack",
      "description": "AMMAN \u2014 Jordan\u2019s military said it had struck \u201cterrorist organizations\u201d in neighboring Syria on Monday in retaliation for a deadly jihadist attack on a church last week, hours after authorities arrested three men they accused of the killings. The military said air force jets had \u201cdestroyed several terrorist positions\u201d and killed and wounded \u201cnumerous members\u201d of groups linked to the perpetrators of the church attack. Syrian authorities reported civilian casualties from the raids, with health officials in southern Daraa province saying six people, including three children and a woman, were killed. Syria\u2019s civil defense agency reported seven deaths in total, six people in Daraa and one in neighboring Sweida province. On Friday, gunmen killed nine people during a funeral service at the Greek Orthodox Saint George\u2019s Church in the village of Hammoud in Karak governorate, southern Jordan. Karak is home to a significant Christian community in Muslim-majority Jordan, where Christians make up between 2 and 3 percent of the population. Maj. Gen. Abdullah al-Maaytah, Jordan\u2019s director of public security, on Monday named the three detained suspects as Hazza Zaidan Mohammed, Meshal Zaidan Mohammed and Ahmad Sami Hussein. The trio \u2014 two of them brothers and the other related by marriage \u2014 were cornered at an abandoned house where they had barricaded themselves inside and opened fire on security forces. \u201cAll three terrorists have been injured. They have been taken to [the] hospital and are still receiving treatment,\u201d Maaytah told a press conference. \u2018We will show them\u2019 One of the suspects was in critical condition, Jordan\u2019s state broadcaster Al-Mamlaka reported. There were no casualties among the security forces, it added. \u201cThis country must remain an oasis of security and stability, and we affirm that Jordan will remain impervious to anyone who dares to challenge it,\u201d Maaytah said. Under Jordanian law, murder is punishable by death by hanging. A number of weapons and a large quantity of ammunition were seized from the three suspects, the Public Security Directorate said. Jordan\u2019s King Abdullah II said in a post on X that security forces \u201cchased them to the very end.\u201d \u201cWe vowed the terrorists would not escape punishment, and no proponent of dark ideologies that divide Jordanians will be tolerated,\u201d he wrote. Earlier, the king visited wounded victims of the church attack in the hospital, dressed in military fatigues and carrying a pistol on his hip. \u201cWe will show them,\u201d he vowed. Interior Minister Mazen al-Faraya told Al-Mamlaka on Monday that the attackers were all Jordanians and \u201chad been holed up in a building in one of the villages near the site of the attack.\u201d \u201cThere is no indication of any external contacts or collaboration with foreign parties,\u201d he said. Government communications chief Mohammad al-Momani said \u201cwe stand ready and will strike with an iron fist, without mercy or leniency, against anyone who attempts to undermine Jordan\u2019s security.\u201d Attacks on houses of worship in Jordan are rare. In 2016, an attack in Karak targeted a security center and police patrols, leaving 10 people dead. On Friday, Prime Minister Jafar Hassan ordered a three-day period of national mourning for the victims of the church attack, with flags to be flown at half-staff over government and public institutions. Eight of the nine victims were laid to rest on Sunday next to the church in a funeral that drew a huge crowd amid a large security presence, AFP images showed. The ninth person, Mohammed al-Qasous, was buried in south Amman on Monday.",
